WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.960
Take a French sentence about volunteering.

2
00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:04.020
«Il y a trois ans»,

3
00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:06.620
it begins, «que je fais du bénévolat».

4
00:00:06.620 --> 00:00:08.430
Word for word, you get:

5
00:00:08.430 --> 00:00:11.740
'There are three years that
I do volunteering.' Every

6
00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:13.590
word is accounted for.

7
00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:16.920
So why would no English
speaker ever say it?

8
00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:19.330
And some French words wear a disguise:

9
00:00:19.330 --> 00:00:22.220
«sensible», «rester», «le collège».

10
00:00:22.220 --> 00:00:23.820
They look English.

11
00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:27.900
Are they? So why does
word-for-word French sound wrong,

12
00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:30.770
and what does a good
translation need instead?

13
00:00:30.770 --> 00:00:35.110
In this mission, you'll map French
tenses onto an English timeline.

14
00:00:35.110 --> 00:00:39.780
Then you'll flip false-friend cards
to see what each word really means.

15
00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:43.160
Your mission: find out what
a translation needs that word

16
00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:44.670
for word can't give.
